@*
    OdsBreakdownTile — a labelled summary tile that lists a small distribution as
    icon · label · count rows (Odyssey Design System · components/BreakdownTile).
    "By type", "By status", "By currency" and the like — the generic form of a
    page Overview's by-type breakdown, so any page can drop one (or several, fed
    different data) into a .odc-summary-grid.

    Pass Label (the overline caption) and Rows — each an OdsBreakdownRow
    { Icon, IconColor, Label, Count, Key }. The count is right-aligned in tabular
    monospace; the icon tints to IconColor. When Rows is empty the Empty message
    shows instead. Styled by .odc-breakdown-*.

    A TOTAL ROW closes the tile, and it is ON BY DEFAULT: a distribution whose sum a reader has
    to add up in their head is a table, not a summary. Total sums the rows' own counts when every
    one is a number; a tile whose counts are NODES (money, a rendered figure) cannot be summed
    safely, so nothing renders rather than a wrong figure — such a tile passes TotalValue instead.

    Total="false" is for a distribution whose sum MEANS nothing, and there are two shapes of that.
    Rows that OVERLAP: a photo sits in several albums and carries several tags, so summing the
    per-tag counts counts one photo many times. And rows that are a SLICE of one another: the
    contracts "Ending soon" row is already inside Active, so an arithmetic sum double-counts it —
    that tile passes the real figure as TotalValue instead. A tile whose rows silently omit a
    bucket the data can hold is the third case: its rows are a partition of what it renders but
    not of what exists, so a total would read as the record count and quietly disagree with it.
*@

<div class="odc-breakdown @Class">
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Label))
    {
        <span class="odc-breakdown-ov">@Label</span>
    }
    @if (Rows.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="odc-breakdown-rows">
            @foreach (var (row, i) in Rows.Select((r, i) => (r, i)))
            {
                <div class="odc-breakdown-row" @key="row.Key ?? i">
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(row.Icon))
                    {
                        <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true"
                              style="@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(row.IconColor) ? null : $"color:{row.IconColor};")">@row.Icon</span>
                    }
                    <span class="odc-breakdown-label">@row.Label</span>
                    <span class="odc-breakdown-n">@row.Count</span>
                </div>
            }
            @if (TotalContent is not null)
            {
                @* The tile's conclusion: ruled off above and muted in its label, so it never reads
                   as another category. *@
                <div class="odc-breakdown-row odc-breakdown-total">
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(TotalIcon))
                    {
                        <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">@TotalIcon</span>
                    }
                    <span class="odc-breakdown-label">@TotalLabel</span>
                    <span class="odc-breakdown-n">@TotalContent</span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="odc-breakdown-empty">@Empty</div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The overline caption (e.g. "By type").</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The distribution rows.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<OdsBreakdownRow> Rows { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Message shown when there are no rows.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Empty { get; set; } = "Nothing to show.";

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether to close the tile with a ruled total row. <b>On by default</b>, matching the design
    /// system. The row shows the sum of every <see cref="OdsBreakdownRow.Count"/> — but only when all
    /// of them are numbers — unless <see cref="TotalValue"/> supplies the figure instead.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Pass <c>false</c> for a distribution whose sum means nothing: overlapping buckets (a photo is
    /// in several albums), rows carrying a slice of one another (the contracts status tile's "Ending
    /// soon" is inside Active, so summing would double-count it — that one passes a real
    /// <see cref="TotalValue"/>), or a tile whose rows leave out a bucket the data can hold.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Total { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// An explicit total, for exactly the case the arithmetic cannot serve: a net, a figure the caller
    /// computed, or rows whose counts are rendered nodes rather than numbers. Takes precedence over
    /// the summed value.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public object? TotalValue { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The total row's label. "Total" unless the figure is something else ("Net").</summary>
    [Parameter] public string TotalLabel { get; set; } = "Total";

    /// <summary>Material Icons ligature for the total row; empty for none.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TotalIcon { get; set; } = "functions";

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the total row renders, or null for no row. An explicit <see cref="TotalValue"/> wins;
    /// otherwise the counts are summed, and only when EVERY one of them is a number — a node count
    /// (money, a coloured figure) has no safe arithmetic, so the row is omitted rather than guessed.
    /// </summary>
    private object? TotalContent
    {
        get
        {
            if (Rows.Count == 0) return null;
            // An explicit figure IS the opt-in — a caller that computed a net has already said it
            // wants the row, and making them pass Total as well would be a second switch for one fact.
            if (TotalValue is not null) return TotalValue;
            if (!Total) return null;

            var sum = 0m;
            foreach (var row in Rows)
            {
                if (!TryNumber(row.Count, out var n)) return null;
                sum += n;
            }
            // Whole counts read as whole counts: the summed form is what the rows themselves are.
            return sum == decimal.Truncate(sum) ? (object)(long)sum : sum;
        }
    }

    private static bool TryNumber(object? value, out decimal number)
    {
        switch (value)
        {
            case null:
                number = 0;
                return false;
            case int i: number = i; return true;
            case long l: number = l; return true;
            case decimal d: number = d; return true;
            case double dd: number = (decimal)dd; return true;
            case string s:
                return decimal.TryParse(s, System.Globalization.NumberStyles.Any,
                    System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out number);
            default:
                number = 0;
                return false;
        }
    }
}
